 "texts": {
  "fields": [
   {
    "name": "uncovered_scene",
    "description": "With empty memory, store the label of the first current execution frame showing the exposed colored cubes. Thereafter copy the incoming label unchanged, including after containers appear. Use the retained image to identify where the cube named by the goal was concealed. e.g. \"E0\"",
    "annotation": "Store the first execution frame showing the colored cubes before containers conceal them. Retain this same label throughout the episode. This preserves the association between the instructed cube color and the subsequently covering container, not a fixed container rank or image-side choice. In all supplied episodes this is E0. e.g. \"E0\"",
    "value_type": "keyframe"
   },
   {
    "name": "button_pressed",
    "description": "With empty memory initialize false. Set true when the current motion frames and robot state show the closed fingers have descended onto the circular button and reached contact, with TCP near z=0.024 m over its center. Otherwise copy the incoming value. Never set true merely because containers appeared, the fingers closed at hover height, or a downward press is being commanded. Once true, retain true. e.g. false",
    "annotation": "Initially false. Change to true at the cycle showing completion of the closed-finger downward press on the button, and retain true thereafter. Contact ends with the TCP approximately 0.024 m above the table even though the commanded press height is 0.0075 m. Thus this field is false at cycles 1\u20133 and true at cycles 4\u20138 in every supplied episode. Record observed completion, not the selection of a future pressing waypoint. e.g. false",
    "value_type": "boolean"
   },
   {
    "name": "completed_pickups",
    "description": "With empty memory initialize to 0. Read the requested color order from the goal. Increment the incoming count once when this cycle's motion frames show the container for the next uncompleted requested color being lifted in a closed grasp to approximately z=0.20 and exposing its cube. Use the incoming count to identify which pickup qualifies. Do not count continued holding or lowering of an already completed pickup, grasp closure alone, or a lift that is only being commanded. Otherwise copy the incoming value, including through return, release, and retreat. e.g. 1",
    "annotation": "The number of pickups completed in the goal's requested order. Initialize to 0. Increment once when the cycle shows the next required container actually lifted in a closed grasp to approximately z=0.20, exposing its cube. Do not increment on grasp closure or on selection of a lift waypoint. A continued hold or lowering of an already counted container does not count again. Retain the count through return, release, and retreat, even when the cube becomes concealed again. e.g. 1",
    "value_type": "count"
   }
  ],
  "action": "Begin with the open hand high above the table and empty memory. Identify the circular gray button on its square base and the cube color specified by the goal. Use calibrated camera geometry, not image-side labels, to estimate world coordinates. The front image's right is world +y. Recover object centers using their physical heights rather than projecting elevated top faces onto the table. The retained uncovered_scene supplies the cube-to-container association after the cubes disappear; compare its cube locations with the current containers, allowing for their top-face height and perspective. Extra containers are distractors. Do not infer that container appearance means the button has been pressed.\n\nApply the following rules to the observed situation. Use current contact evidence together with incoming button_pressed to recognize that the press has occurred; the outgoing memory is not a prerequisite for recognizing current contact. Likewise, use incoming completed_pickups together with any qualifying lift completion observed this cycle to determine the number of completed pickups for action selection. A commanded lift is not a completed pickup. Read the requested color order from the goal; with an empty hand, select the next uncompleted color. Never repeat a completed pickup or the completed button press.\n\nButton-hover arrival tolerance determines which operation is next, not the button's coordinates. For every button approach, closing, pressing, opening, and retraction command, reconstruct the circular button's actual world center from calibrated current imagery at its physical height. Never substitute the measured hover TCP x,y for that center. Closing at hover uses that center at z=0.15; pressing uses that center at z=0.0075. Preserve the nonidentical-waypoint safeguard for gripper-only changes.\n\nBefore pressing: if the hand is not at the button hover, command [button_x, button_y, 0.15, -3.1416, 0, 0] with gripper open. Treat an approach ending within roughly 1.5 cm horizontally and 1 cm vertically as arrival. If at this hover with open fingers, command the same nominal hover with gripper closed. If at hover with closed fingers and button_pressed is false, command [button_x, button_y, 0.0075, -3.1416, 0, 0] with gripper closed. Do not repeatedly drive downward when the fingers have contacted the button: the realized TCP may stop near z=0.024 rather than the commanded height.\n\nAfter observed button contact, or with button_pressed true: if still low over the button, retract to [button_x, button_y, 0.15, -3.1416, 0, 0], keeping the gripper closed. At the button hover with closed fingers, open the gripper at that nominal hover. At the button hover with open fingers, move directly to the target-container hover, not back down onto the button.\n\nThe target is the container covering the next uncompleted requested color in uncovered_scene. Associate the remembered cube locations with current containers using calibrated world coordinates, not raw image-centroid overlap. Reconstruct the visible square top's corners by intersecting calibrated camera rays with its top/grasp plane, approximately z=0.0633\u20130.0634. Compute the world-plane center from these corners and derive yaw from the reconstructed world-plane edges, not image-plane slopes. Choose a downward orientation [3.1416, 0, yaw] aligned with opposing grasp faces, using the equivalent square-aligned yaw closest to zero. Refine the estimate with current wrist geometry, distinguishing the optical center from the TCP projection. Use this reconstructed container center for both the open-hand hover [target_x, target_y, 0.20, 3.1416, 0, yaw] and the descent to z approximately 0.0634 with closure on arrival. When already at the target hover with open fingers, descend rather than repeating the hover. Do not copy an offset realized hover position into the grasp command or substitute the remembered cube's image centroid for the elevated container's current top center. Apply the held-container return and release-clearance rules before any next-target approach or grasp.\n\nWhen the fingers have closed around the target at grasp height, typically leaving about 0.018 m on each finger, lift to z=0.20 over the same grasp center with the same yaw and gripper closed. Do not treat selection of this waypoint as completion.\n\nWhen the required container has actually reached approximately z=0.20 in a closed grasp and another requested pickup remains, return it over its original cube: command the current measured x,y at z=0.07, preserving the current orientation, and open on arrival. This return-and-release rule takes priority over approaching the next target. Preserve the completed pickup count even when the returned container conceals its cube.\n\nWith open fingers low over a returned, already completed container, retract vertically to approximately z=0.145 at the current x,y and orientation, keeping the gripper open. This release-clearance rule takes priority over grasp rules and the generic hover fallback. Once at this clearance height, move directly to the next uncompleted container's z=0.20 hover. Then descend, close, and lift according to the target rules. When the last requested pickup has actually been lifted, retain the closed grasp; do not return or release it. Never try to grasp a revealed cube.\n\nFor commands that only change the gripper, use the nominal hover target if it differs from the measured TCP by at least 2 mm. Otherwise add a 3 mm upward displacement so the waypoint is not identical to the current pose. Small execution errors do not imply a new task stage or require replaying an already completed press.\n\nIf alignment is uncertain, use the retained scene and current wrist/front geometry to refine the relevant object's location and make a corrective open-hand hover motion before descending. If a commanded motion explicitly executed no steps, preserve memory and retry the applicable rule with a reachable, nonidentical waypoint. For an otherwise unmatched situation, safely raise the hand vertically to the relevant hover height, preserving a held object's closed grip, then reapply these observation-based rules. Do not wait without a specific observable purpose."
 },
